update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('639264bc-735b-463a-881c-f12c4be3e025'::uuid,'89653a512c7360fedea8e508439e98ea','A pushes the wrong way. With less funding, fire departments should fight drought fires less effectively, so each drought fire should do more damage, not less. Drought already brings more fires; add weaker firefighting and drought damage should be even higher. That deepens the paradox.'),
('c16b668d-57be-45c5-8d84-533ce521336a'::uuid,'39ac34922ff4005ed1e24d83d0ffb2d0','B compares places, not periods. It says fire-prone areas are less densely populated than areas with few fires. The paradox is about the same areas in drought versus normal rainfall. Population density does not change when the rain stops, so it cannot explain why the damage changes.'),
('d30c5889-77cc-4ea4-85bf-71c8445103de'::uuid,'e0941303e9094338cb1e3f4927dcd3d7','C gives us the difference-maker. Large, hard-to-control fires, the expensive kind, typically occur only when there is a lot of vegetation to consume. Add the common sense link from the setup: rain grows vegetation, and a long drought leaves much less of it.

Now both facts fit. In a drought, fires start easily, so there are many more of them, but there is little fuel, so they stay small. In normal rainfall, fires are rarer, but the ones that start have plenty of fuel and can grow into the large blazes that do the heavy damage. A few big fires can cost more than many small ones.

The move to repeat: when more of something produces less total harm, look for a reason each one got smaller. The right answer changes the size of each event, not the count.'),
('d7d80a20-4912-4bbf-8427-9801ad40dde9'::uuid,'c33b21ffbcbdd97082fd6caaae37ad46','D tells us that fires not caused by negligence or arson tend to be started by lightning. Nothing in that changes between drought and normal rainfall, and nothing in it says how big a fire gets once it starts. How a fire begins tells us nothing about damage per fire, which is the gap we need to explain.'),
('c55a4068-20ef-428c-a31f-0a7159c0eebf'::uuid,'b25b3e19502109ba27a89c6c7ffef579','E is the vegetation trap. It talks about fuel, which is the right topic, but it does not help. If burned vegetation comes back equally or more flammable, the land stays as easy to burn as before. That gives no reason for drought fires to be smaller, and it draws no line between drought and normal periods. Compare C: C ties fire size to how much vegetation there is, which is exactly what rainfall changes. E only describes what grows back.'),
('fd725bcc-aae7-4a96-bab4-cdee55d6e90f'::uuid,'202f0f17b8a978f790ce644d84dde674','A tells us the squads are hard to staff. That might mean the squads save fewer people than they could, but abolishing them saves no one at all. Test it: according to A, would climbers act any differently without the squads? Nothing says so, so A gives no route to a lower toll.'),
('7d44c74d-f118-4dc2-bda5-aca5deb463b9'::uuid,'bfe3ba56a8143133f189780d6f0c7b57','B is tempting because it seems to explain the numbers. If squad regions record deaths and injuries more accurately, abolishing the squads might lower the recorded toll. But the climbers believe the toll itself can be reduced, meaning fewer people actually hurt or killed. Sloppier counting saves no one. B explains a statistic, not the belief.'),
('cdb89b2e-d774-4c86-b6a8-41259e0e4723'::uuid,'4f07bc3fbc2e2166f3f192750d4c5ff0','C says risk-takers do not expect anyone to come rescue them. If so, the squads are not what makes them take risks, and removing the squads would not make them any more careful. They would climb the same way and simply lose the rescues. That makes abolishing the squads look worse, the opposite of what we need.'),
('19e4f3f2-6727-40ff-b8ac-b3bb9f6439db'::uuid,'e697cdf5f7215a11c00dad447db71283','D describes the victims: most were not adequately prepared. But it never connects that to the squads. Why are unprepared climbers on the mountain? If the answer were that the squads make climbing feel safe, we would have a resolution, but D never says that. Without that link, removing the squads changes nothing D describes.'),
('2e928907-d966-43b1-ac7f-0ea3881e17c7'::uuid,'4047a9196e1e4e83e785c0c41859795c','E supplies the missing behavior change. E never mentions the squads, so we add the obvious link: a rescue squad makes a mountain seem less risky. E says that the lower the perceived risk, the more less competent climbers attempt the climb. So the squads draw in exactly the climbers most likely to get hurt.

Run it backward. Abolish the squads, the mountains feel more dangerous, and fewer less competent climbers try. Fewer people in over their heads can mean fewer deaths and injuries overall, even with no one left to rescue those who do get in trouble.

The move to repeat: when someone claims a safety measure causes more harm, look for how it changes behavior. A safety net can pull more people into the risk.'),
('bc1e9cd3-871c-463a-ae9e-14108e66a53e'::uuid,'6963688863a9f4b1bd18e4caa6760554','A takes road 1. If the wastewater draws in fish that prey on bass, more bass get eaten and fewer are left downstream to catch. That explains the drop in bass caught, so A is out.'),
('09715b96-d8dc-43ac-9da7-8df2738d6c9d'::uuid,'36b237f8f43a4e352d60291924071fac','B also takes road 1. The warmer discharge sends bass off to find cooler water elsewhere. Fewer bass stay downstream of the plant, so anglers there catch fewer. That explains the drop, so eliminate B.'),
('a7363b92-0e04-4d9f-92f5-559a26c2300a'::uuid,'fb4ffc92331aa08bc7ea696c80dc24a9','C pushes the other way. Better access brings more anglers fishing for bass downstream. More people fishing should mean more bass caught, not fewer, so C cannot explain the decrease.

Could you rescue C by imagining the extra anglers fished the bass out? Only by inventing that story yourself, and it runs against what C actually tells us. C''s own effect is more people fishing, and on its face that means more bass caught. The four other answers each point straight at fewer bass caught; C points the other way. That is what marks the answer that does not belong.'),
('96597a73-05b1-4189-b7a8-85bb2b514718'::uuid,'90bdfbd1dad314b7f2d5b66d4843b515','D is the road 2 answer, and it is easy to throw out as off topic because it is about anglers, not bass. The bass may be fine. But noise makes that stretch of river a less popular place to fish, and fewer anglers catch fewer bass. That is exactly the decrease described, so D explains it and is out.'),
('6b186f39-db7c-4cdb-811b-9d07def23aa5'::uuid,'c2f7a9c6eba2c67409143ebb32cb2fc9','E is road 1 again. Turbulence from the wastewater disrupted the river vegetation and destroyed some of the bass''s habitat. Less habitat supports fewer bass downstream, so fewer get caught. E explains the drop.'),
('dfc41b19-dd36-46d8-b6cb-a4e8fae00a46'::uuid,'a3882a896e36bbc6cff3522ebce636ed','A explains nothing. It says the new heater uses a smaller percentage of the household''s gas than the old one did. A percentage is a share, not an amount, so A does not tell us whether total gas use went up, or why.

Try numbers. Old heater: 50% of 100 units, so 50 units. New heater at 40%: if the household still uses 100 units, the heater uses 40; if the household now uses 150, the heater uses 60. A fits either story because it only describes how the gas is split. It gives no extra gas use and no higher price, so it is the one answer that does not contribute. Careful: A does not even say the new heater burns less gas than the old one, so it does not deepen the puzzle either. It simply gives no cause.'),
('9f68be7b-734d-4880-baf5-c9d681c84059'::uuid,'12143de9d256920a7bcfb56686888921','B is route one. Jimmy''s uncle moved in shortly after the installation, doubling the household. Twice as many people means more hot water and more gas burned overall. More gas used raises the bill even with an efficient heater, so B helps explain the increase.'),
('37408eef-a873-4ef6-975c-86786a866e8a'::uuid,'b3f67c7108d6a34ab67e297c715ee6ac','C is route one through a new appliance. Jimmy started using a gas dryer when the heater was replaced. Before that, the laundry was done at a laundromat, so this gas use is brand new to the household. It helps explain the higher bill.'),
('1139d3e6-47bd-485f-a0b3-8f09b9d53771'::uuid,'fa3fc0eefcdab976425c33da77296bcc','D is route two: price. If the utility raised its rates after the installation, Jimmy pays more for each unit of gas. The bill can rise even if the household burns less gas than before. D helps explain the increase, so eliminate it.'),
('e5a42386-31c9-48c0-9d8d-d1b763f5e930'::uuid,'a255c9e8f5f4e8eb2f376c6da9a8d04e','E is route one through heating. Unusually cold weather after the installation led to heavy gas usage. That extra gas has nothing to do with the water heater''s efficiency, and it raises the bill. E helps explain the increase.'),
('59ef34b9-9fb1-479a-a40f-25c01c9e601b'::uuid,'a0cc43cc39943a9b72b7072f34156d6a','A is the cost route. When computer technology changes fast, unsold stock is more likely to become obsolete. Obsolete stock gets marked down or written off, and that loss comes out of the store''s profit on PC sales. A helps explain the thin margin, so eliminate it.'),
('79623cef-512b-446b-96cd-11684bf1a3db'::uuid,'fba2a94b4a6f9429761f04e4e02853be','B is the exception. Brand loyalty is loyalty to a maker''s brand, not to a store. Run the two-route test. Does B lower the price a store can charge? No. Does it raise what the store spends to make a sale? No. B also never compares PCs with the other high-tech items, so it gives no reason the PC margin in particular should be low.'),
('a896e35d-6599-48a6-8f65-b37a66ffc9b0'::uuid,'09d413adfe423533da37617773a6b769','C is the cost route, with the comparison built in. If PC buyers need more help from store employees than buyers of other high-tech items, each PC sale costs the store more in staff time. Higher cost per sale means a lower margin, and it explains the gap with other high-tech items too.'),
('b4dc49ba-f562-409f-a103-72ef40f95db8'::uuid,'b569be71c6aebfd306c8dbe2ee88bcda','D is the price route, chosen on purpose. Stores keep PC prices low to bring in customers who might buy software and accessories. The PC works as a loss leader: the store accepts a thin margin on the computer to make money on the extras. That explains the low PC margin directly.'),
('bde5d3d7-5cfb-4147-aa07-9ae07af49de2'::uuid,'f3cc26e9cb645ae0d633518a05212b27','E is the price route through competition. More discount outlets selling PCs means more stores fighting for the same customers, and that fight is usually won on price. Lower prices squeeze margins even while demand keeps rising. E helps explain the discrepancy.'),
('538fa497-ea20-4002-965c-fab1c83cfbe7'::uuid,'08e893349d986c35b68bba84fab150ef','A works through focus. Repressors are better able to focus on their work and avoid distractions. Better focus on schoolwork is a direct reason for higher grades. One explained advantage is enough on this question, so A contributes and is out.'),
('652f882b-50c0-499d-b058-48e084f89d3b'::uuid,'b2fa1b702890cb49c5b29d149ec82449','B works through relationships. Repressors are less apt to alienate people by expressing their emotions. Fewer alienated classmates helps explain superior social skills, and plausibly less shyness and more self-esteem. B contributes, so eliminate it.'),
('97f9630a-42e9-40a1-b0ac-c74018e72d8d'::uuid,'37b9373642bf852dc95910c262901378','C works from outside the students. Parents and other caregivers reward repressors more than sensitizers for "academic performance and social behavior deemed desirable." Rewarded behavior tends to be repeated, and being rewarded tends to build self-esteem. That helps explain the higher grades, the social skills and the self-esteem. C contributes.'),
('c088e7e8-9291-4ff3-8bec-3414c8da0415'::uuid,'e9177263b98273dfcdde719538368484','D runs the wrong direction. It reports a hypothesis that the desire to maintain social and academic success and self-esteem strengthens the tendency to repress. That is a reason repressors repress more, not a reason repressing produces the advantages. A desire to maintain success already assumes the success is there; D takes the advantages as given and explains something else.

Even if that hypothesis were correct, we would still have no account of why repressors have higher grades or better social skills.

The move to repeat: on an explain-the-result EXCEPT question, ask what each answer actually explains. An answer can be on topic and still explain the wrong thing.'),
('25aad0db-81e7-46f5-b037-becbdb16a1ef'::uuid,'fec75eda131a56eb31fe2578ba40edb9','E works from the sensitizer side. Sensitizers focus more on the difficulties of succeeding than on what is likely to bring success. Dwelling on obstacles plausibly breeds anxiety and lower self-esteem. The finding is a comparison, so a reason sensitizers fall behind is also a reason repressors look better. E contributes.'),
('032af2ef-7b04-4e8a-b8ac-06089eb4a686'::uuid,'4755749cd1317feb02a944acd40721b5','A matches piece for piece. Rule: has a dog → knows the true value of companionship. Fact: Alicia has demonstrated knowing the true value of companionship, the right side. Conclusion: Alicia has a dog, the left side. That is the original''s reversal exactly: observe the result, conclude the trigger. Alicia might value companionship because of friends, family or a cat.

The move to repeat: on a conditional parallel flaw question, diagram the rule, then check that the evidence and the conclusion land on the same sides of the arrow as in the original.'),
('9d03fdbc-0075-4134-ab8c-540b5bc04a4e'::uuid,'098cc2732a71cf4c1f11cd02e9e77e24','B diagrams as discovers something new → examined all possible solutions. Fran never discovered something new, which negates the left side, and the conclusion negates the right side. That is denying the trigger: a flaw, but not the original''s, which affirmed the result. B also slides from examined to explored.'),
('84486f6b-e004-407a-845f-94f513d0e5b5'::uuid,'e9a73ddb5e38dd8bd2836a70f15e1472','C is the topic lure: challenges and accomplishing everything sound like the original. Diagram it. Not sufficient challenges → does not accomplish everything one can. Jill accomplishes everything, which negates the right side, so Jill faces sufficient challenges, the negated left side. That is a valid contrapositive, and a parallel flaw answer has to be flawed.'),
('54a2433e-0e48-40b7-84a2-271439ee6fb7'::uuid,'e258f316a0dd3a982194518fcbe92312','D starts from a definition, and a definition runs both ways: every polygon is a closed plane figure bounded by straight lines, and every such figure is a polygon. So moving from the description to the label is valid. A large number of straight lines is still straight lines. There is no flaw here to match.'),
('55cf07b4-a8ee-4104-b320-c9c80e8aef00'::uuid,'a9158f6a7e32427672bccf4fa65c98c7','E is the closest trap, because it also seems to move from a result to a trigger. But the pieces do not line up. The rule''s result is being lax about keeping property secure; the evidence shows Jon lax about property Jon can afford to lose, a narrower claim. The rule''s trigger is never having lost something one cannot afford to lose; the conclusion is that Jon never lost anything at all. Both pieces drift from the rule''s terms, so E is not the clean reversal the original makes. A copies it exactly.'),
('bf55905a-48db-495d-8ee7-27f8334b35f5'::uuid,'d9e2a0723f094a2a06b6d52997fd8ca2','A is the same recipe. People who invest heavily in the stock market generally have higher incomes than people who do not: a group comparison. So if you want a high income, invest heavily: advice built on that comparison. And it has the same hole. A high income may be what lets people invest heavily in the first place, just as an expected big refund may be what makes people hire a tax service. Even the conclusion''s wording matches the original: if you want Y, you should do X.'),
('ead5f503-0a78-408d-97f3-f72480df887c'::uuid,'5476492ddb3e24bcbf5f6aa291bb071f','B''s evidence is already causal: an in-house print shop usually enables companies to produce publications more efficiently. If X enables Y, recommending X to companies that make publications is not a jump from correlation to cause. Whatever looseness B has, it is not the original''s flaw.'),
('c94129c1-0034-49f3-acec-32076a1d97bf'::uuid,'c7f949e3acb38664f65ebe8ef302d497','C commits a different error. Quitting smoking causes two things: a lower life insurance premium and a lower lung cancer risk. The argument then says to reduce your cancer risk to lower your premium, as if one effect produced the other. That confuses two effects of one cause. There is no comparison between groups at all.'),
('03a3c0e1-d0b4-4b86-b4a2-c363b3b0d3ce'::uuid,'ccc918b322c395028702f00754a68bbe','D''s evidence already says the lawyer helps: people with larger-than-average estates usually find it helpful to have their own lawyers. Advising someone with such an estate to get a lawyer follows from that. There is no group comparison and no hidden causal leap, so there is no matching flaw.'),
('13927832-afa8-42fe-8e51-df8a5df0349b'::uuid,'2aec4055a98d9b1b01a2e197ec0e1071','E''s evidence describes how telemarketers choose whom to call: they target people who have bought from them before. That is a stated cause. Buying puts you on the call list, so not buying should mean fewer calls. The advice follows from the mechanism the evidence gives, so the original''s correlation flaw is missing.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;